INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Wirecard KPMG announcement and European Parliament/SAFE policy briefing", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002", "text": "SRC-001 (Wirecard announcement, 28 April 2020): Wirecard said KPMG's special audit found no incriminating evidence for public allegations of balance-sheet forgery and no significant findings requiring adjustment of 2016-2018 annual accounts across TPA, MCA/Digital Lending, India and Singapore. It stated that, under KPMG's increased forensic requirements, not all data needed to prove 2016-2018 TPA revenues could be obtained because data were primarily controlled by third parties; more than 200 million December 2019 data sets were supplied and no deviations between reported revenues and account balances were found. KPMG identified documentation and organizational weaknesses; Wirecard said it was remedying them since 2019. Wirecard also said publication of annual accounts and annual press conference would be delayed, pending coordination with EY.\n\nSRC-002 (European Parliament ECON-commissioned policy briefing, manuscript completed May 2020 / published November 2020): Authors assess that all five lines of defence failed in some way: Wirecard internal control, supervisory board, external audit, financial-reporting/audit oversight bodies, and BaFin. The briefing says warning signals existed years before revelation. It recounts allegations from 2015, the 2016 Zatarra report, FT reporting and whistleblower information, BaFin's January 2019 request for FREP investigation, its temporary net-short-sale restriction and complaint against short sellers/journalist, Wirecard's October 2019 hiring of KPMG, and KPMG's delayed April 2020 report saying it could not verify existence of €1bn in foreign bank accounts. It says BaFin filed a criminal complaint in June 2020 and, days later, Wirecard filed for bankruptcy acknowledging €1.9bn in missing financial assets. The authors characterize the root cause as flawed market/institutional oversight architecture, while warning against hindsight bias and describing the ultimate causes as a mix of institutional deficiencies and possible perpetrator failures. The source text returned contains no information about Jan Marsalek's disappearance."}
